and a 13-year-old in the ability to do those things. So you mentioned a minute ago about disparities and what happens to children who come from families that don't have the resources to manage their education at home and those who do. So if schools are forced into remote learning, what can they do to address these equity issues? Some of it is purely access, which doesn't go into really the details of learning at all, but it is purely looking at, and schools did a lot of this in spring 2020 with the big closures, providing devices if you knew that kids didn't have them at home, providing in some places, school systems knew that there wasn't good broadband access and they might have done packets, right, instead of online. Some did mobile hotspots. You know, we read about, I'm sure people have read about buses where they put, you know, hot spots on the bus and drove them around or people driving to parking lots, you know, next to a library where they would have. None of that is ideal.
和十三岁在做这些事情的能力上有很大的不同。所以你刚才提到了差异，以及那些来自没有资源在家管理教育的家庭的孩子与那些有资源的孩子之间会发生什么。那么，如果学校被迫进行远程学习，他们能做些什么来解决这些公平问题呢？其中一些纯粹是接入问题，这根本不涉及学习细节，但纯粹是着眼于此。学校在2020年春季大范围关闭时做了很多工作，比如如果你知道孩子们家里没有设备，就提供设备；在某些地方，学校系统知道没有良好的宽带接入，他们可能会提供学习包，而不是在线学习。有些学校提供了移动热点。你知道，我们读到过，我相信人们读到过关于校车的新闻，他们在校车上安装热点并开着它们到处走，或者人们开车到图书馆旁边的停车场，在那里他们会有信号。这些都不是理想的。

